[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: iPad screen showing video editing software with "EDITING" overlay.
AUDIO: 'I found an old tube camera in my garage from the 1980s and it actually works. I\'m using it right now. I\'m kidding. It\'s a new app. Meet the Aaron Cam app.'

[00:12] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hand moving in front of a camera, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'Or is it the A-A-Ron Cam app? &gt;&gt; A-A-Ron. Where is A-A-Ron right now? &gt;&gt; Aaron Cam turns your iPhone into a late 70s analog video tube camera by'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'faithfully modeling the physics of this old technology. Capture video with those gorgeous light trails and weird purple damage streaks you have seen in archival footage or may even remember from your childhood.'

[00:38] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A vintage Sony Video 8 Pro camera with a battery.
AUDIO: 'I have to say, it\'s one of the best old school video emulators I\'ve used. I personally never had a video tube camera, but I did have a very early Sony video 8 camera that my grandfather gave me back in the mid-80s. So, it was a'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'similar SD resolution, but it used a CCD which eliminated the image burn-in and trails of the video tube cameras. Here\'s more behind the idea of this app from the guy who developed it and yep,'

[01:03] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Vintage-style footage of a child jumping on a bed.
AUDIO: 'his name is Aaron. &gt;&gt; My parents got me an old RCA CC004 at a garage sale when I was seven. And I went from making Little Red Riding Hood &gt;&gt; Better to eat you. &gt;&gt; to co-founding MasterClass and'

[01:15] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A small screen displaying blue-tinted vintage video, with text about "purple damage the video tubes".
AUDIO: 'outlier.org. Sadly, these cameras are aging poorly. Many barely work even when I get them in new condition. They\'re fully analog and function nothing like a modern video camera. So, I set out to replicate them on an iPhone by actually modeling their physical and electrical'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'properties rather than reproducing just the visual effects like prior attempts. I was nostalgic for these green light trails and purple damage the video tubes in the camera produced. &gt;&gt; Often when we talk about retro camera apps, we think of vintage Super 8 mm film. And those can be a lot of fun, but'

[01:40] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Footage of chandeliers with light trails and color distortion, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'doing old school video has its own nostalgic aesthetic that I really like. But evidently, this was not an easy app'

[01:55] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Black and white footage of busts on pedestals, with "AaronCam" text.
AUDIO: 'to create. It ended up being much more complicated than I thought it would be to get it working. Took a year of dabbling, several fried cameras, and yielded some fancy patent pending signal processing. So, you can see side by side how by building a physics model of the video'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tube, the app naturally reproduces the visual effects of the real thing. The only way to do this was to run the iPhone at 240 frames per second to mimic an analog response. I accumulated those frames as electrical influence on a virtual photo conductive layer, and then the image is produced by scanning that'

[02:20] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A split screen showing an iPhone holding the AaronCam app and a close-up of its UI.
AUDIO: 'layer from the simulated video tube. A lot of that info is above my pay grade. But in the end, what matters is it works and looks great. The app\'s interface? Well, it\'s very simple and easy to use. You\'ve got the record button,'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a zoom lever, and a lens picker. You can also do selfie shots, like the'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'way I opened the video. Then you\'ve got an indoor or outdoor setting for the white balance, and that\'s it. Just like the old cameras had. Then you\'ve got BLC, which is backlight'

[03:06] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Close-up of a person's face in vintage video style, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'compensation, and forces a higher exposure. There are three settings, low, medium, and high. And it works. I\'ve been using this when shooting in low light.'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then there\'s a settings tab that lets you choose the tube type, brand new or old. The main difference I see is brand new is more colorful, and old is desaturated, much less color.'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Although, there\'s probably some other stuff going on there, too. Now, here are two shots, one using the old tube look, and then the other using the brand new tube look. You can see the difference mainly in how'

[03:44] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Close-up of a wall clock in vintage video style, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'much saturation there is. And then there\'s a CRT effect, which adds scan lines over the footage like it\'s being watched on an old TV. I like that look and used it a lot in my sample footage.'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And yeah, you can shoot with this app in dark settings or at night, too. Well, sort of anyway. I found that the 24 mm really is the'

[04:08] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Low-light footage of an indoor scene with light trails, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'only one that works and you need a good amount of light. But you do get those great light trails. Now, you might be thinking, couldn\'t you'

[04:28] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Outdoor footage of people by a lake, with soft, vintage aesthetics, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'just do this effect in post? Yeah, probably. But it definitely wouldn\'t be as easy and it\'s unlikely it would look as real. I hate how in some movies when they show old video or old film, you can tell that it was just shot'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on an Alexa, a modern-day camera, and they did a post effect that looks way too clean and ultimately fake. In a perfect world, you would use the real thing, whatever video camera or film camera that is. But if you don\'t'

[04:53] [scene:title]
VISUAL: Black screen with "Footage Specs" text.
AUDIO: 'have that, then using a dedicated app like this to emulate it in camera is a great option. And to me, it just feels way more authentic. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Speaking of authentic, the camera shoots'

[05:05] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Vintage footage of a lake, with "640x480" resolution displayed.
AUDIO: 'at 30 frames per second. Again, mimicking the old video camera frame rates of the day. But the files it produces are bigger than SD resolution. They are 2048 by 1536. At least most of them are.'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I did have some files captured at 640 by 480. And so, I\'m not sure if it\'s a bug, but every CRT shot I did was at full resolution. And the ones without that effect on were smaller size.'

[05:31] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Shaky footage from a moving vehicle, with "No Stabilization" text.
AUDIO: 'Not a big deal since we\'re emulating vintage video anyway, but I do prefer the higher resolution for editing. If you\'re going straight to social media though, not that big of a deal. One thing to keep in mind though is'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there is There\'s stabilization. Or if there is, it\'s very minimal, and there\'s not a setting for it. But again, that\'s how old cameras looked, and it\'s really part of the charm. One nice touch they added that I like as well, the file names tell you what lens'

[05:56] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An iPhone displaying the AaronCam app interface.
AUDIO: 'you shot with, and if it has the CRT effect on or not. That\'s pretty cool, and really helps when you\'re logging your footage or getting your footage in the editing app. So, this probably isn\'t an app you\'re going to use all the time. I use it to'

[06:08] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Footage of a chandelier with pronounced light trails and artifacts, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'shoot quasi home movies, just having fun. And I do think it is a lot of fun, and it will be a valuable addition to a mobile filmmaking kit. Oh, and did I mention that it\'s free? It is, at least right now.'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the developer Aaron says he might make a pro version, too, in the future. So, if you want to shoot some nostalgic-looking home movies, retro B-roll, social media kind of stuff, or need to emulate a vintage video look for'

[06:34] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A person taking a selfie in a mirror, with vintage video effects and scan lines, "Shot on AaronCam" watermark.
AUDIO: 'a film or TV project, [music] then yeah, I do recommend checking out the Aaron Cam app. And by the way, a quick shout-out and a thank you to John Duncan, who brought this app to my attention on Twitter, or X, whatever you want to call it.'

